<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Smolensk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Smolensk is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> River, long contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Muscovy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a> in 1618 confirmed Polish possession of Smolensk. This represented the maximum territorial achievement of the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/smolensk.jpg" alt="Smolensk" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ghirlandajo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smolensk_downtown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late fifteenth century the main enemy was Muscovy, which won the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500 and took <b>Smolensk</b> in 1514, despite the Lithuanian victory under Konstanty Ostrogski at the Battle of Orsha the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasily III captured <b>Smolensk</b> in 1514, although a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated his forces at the Battle of Orsha the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Towns grew rapidly: Kiev, Novgorod, Chernigov, <b>Smolensk</b>, Polotsk and Vladimir became centres of crafts and commerce, and Kiev under Yaroslav the Wise was one of the largest cities in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Truce of Deulino of 1618, which confirmed Polish possession of <b>Smolensk</b> and Chernigov, marked the greatest territorial gains of Commonwealth arms in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Time of Troubles Polish forces occupied Moscow from 1610 until a national militia led by Kuzma Minin and Prince Dmitry Pozharsky expelled them in 1612, and the peace treaties of Stolbovo (1617) and Deulino (1618) cost Russia its Baltic coast and <b>Smolensk</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaroslav the Wise assigned his sons principalities in order of seniority: Kiev and Novgorod to the eldest, Iziaslav; Chernigov and Tmutarakan to Sviatoslav Yaroslavich; Pereyaslavl and Rostov-Suzdal to Vsevolod; <b>Smolensk</b> to Vyacheslav and Volhynia to Igor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li></ul></section>
